<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Callatis</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 72 BCE (approx.) · capital Callatis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Dorian colony of Heraclea Pontica on the Dobruja coast, a grain-trading city that defied Lysimachus and later allied with Rome, at modern Mangalia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Callatis was founded by colonists from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/heraclea-pontica">Heraclea Pontica</a> on the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast, probably in the late 6th century BCE, though some sources place the foundation later. Its fertile hinterland in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a> made it a natural centre for grain exports, and it grew alongside the Milesian colonies of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/histria">Histria</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tomis">Tomis</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/odessos">Odessos</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Callatis kept the Doric dialect and institutions of its mother city, with cults of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>. It traded grain, wine and craft goods with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/getae">Getae</a> of the interior and struck its own coinage. In 313 BCE it led the Pontic cities in revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>, withstanding a long siege that ended around 309 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 1st century BCE Callatis concluded a formal treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, a rare surviving text of its kind. After the campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-terentius-varro-lucullus">Marcus Terentius Varro Lucullus</a> in 72-71 BCE it lay within the Roman sphere and later in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a>. The site is the modern Romanian town of Mangalia.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 500 BCE – 72 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:49.75%;width:8.52%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 72 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Callatis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek (Doric)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kallatis, Mangalia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-286&amp;focus=callatis" aria-label="Callatis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5366 625 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Callatis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5686 881L5767 961M5686 881L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5767 961h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5686 881h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5686 881h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-286&amp;focus=callatis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
